// Solid level objects — the test block, and the vertical spring.
//
// TWO OBJECTS IN ONE FILE, AND IT IS NOT WHERE THE SPRING WANTS TO LIVE. A new
// `.emp` module is also a new SECTION, and a new section needs a row in sigil's
// `crates/sigil-harness/src/section_align.rs` (`required_for` returns None for an
// undeclared head label and the caller REFUSES the build naming the section) —
// i.e. a paired sigil landing and a rebuild of the shared `sigil` binary every
// other lane in the suite is currently linking against. The spring is a fun
// sidestep, not a cross-repo parcel, so it rides in the nearest already-declared
// section instead: `test_solid`, whose head label `TestSolid_Init` MUST therefore
// stay the first emitting item in this file (map.toml's `order` and
// section_align both key on it). Splitting the spring into
// `games/sonic4/objects/spring.emp` is booked in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md.
//
// Base fields for both objects are set by Load_Object from their ObjDefs.
module games.sonic4.test_solid in test_solid

use engine.objects.sst.{Sst, ObjDef}
use engine.objects.objdef.{objdef, vram_art}
use engine.objects.mapping_dsl.{MapPiece, MapFrame1, centered, piece}
use engine.constants.{AF_END, AF_CHANGE, AF_SET_FIELD, COLLISION_SPRING, ST_ROLLING, ST_XFLIP}
use games.sonic4.constants.{VRAM_TEST_OBJ, PSTATE_AIR, PSTATE_AIRBALL}
use games.sonic4.player_common.{PlayerV}

// -----------------------------------------------
// TestSolid_Init — set mapping frame from subtype
// In:  a0 = SST pointer (base fields set by Load_Object)
// Clobbers: d0-d3, a1 — the init frame falls
//   into TestSolid_Main (Draw_Sprite's set), so a caller sees Main's clobbers —
//   the old clobbers() under-declared (matches TestParticle's whole-object set).
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc TestSolid_Init (a0: *Sst) clobbers(d0-d3/a1) falls_into TestSolid_Main {
        move.b  subtype(a0), mapping_frame(a0)
        jbsr    RefreshSpritePieceCount         // mapping_frame changed → re-cache
                                                // piece count + frame_off (d2/a1
                                                // dead here, both in our clobbers)
        // The dispatch-store value: this object's Main routine as an offset from
        // the object-code-bank base (engine.objects.objcodebase's ObjCodeBase),
        // a `.w` link-time immediate. `TestSolid_Main` is this module's own
        // `pub proc` (exports unmangled); `ObjCodeBase` is a cross-module symbol —
        // both resolve through the shared link.
        move.w  #TestSolid_Main - ObjCodeBase, code_addr(a0)
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// TestSolid_Main — display-only (collision handled by TouchResponse)
// In:  a0 = SST pointer
// Clobbers: d0-d3, a1 (Draw_Sprite's set — tail call). Declared as the contract
//   the signature under-declared (nothing) while
//   tail-calling Draw_Sprite — the dangerous direction under exhaustive-license.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc TestSolid_Main (a0: *Sst) clobbers(d0-d3/a1) preserves(a0) {
        jbra    Draw_Sprite
}

// ===========================================================================
// THE SPRING — S3K's vertical red spring, on this engine's own collision path
// ===========================================================================
// The PHYSICS is S3K's, cited below to file and line at skdisasm revision
// 2fcd861c208f342b6d14df694c6422c74f20a4be. The CODE is not: solidity lives in
// engine/objects/collision.emp's Touch_Spring, which splices the same
// face-resolution template Touch_Solid does, so the side and underside faces of
// a spring are the same code as the side and underside of a block. This file
// holds only what is Sonic-4's: the launch's meaning for the player state
// machine, the animation, the mapping frames and the archetype.
//
// SUBTYPE-DRIVEN SINCE 2026-09-06 — direction and strength come out of the
// object's subtype byte, on S3K's bit assignments. See "THE SUBTYPE BIT LAYOUT"
// below for the decode read off the reference; Spring_Init is where it happens.
//
// FOUR DIRECTIONS SINCE 2026-09-06 (SP-5) — up, down, left and right. The
// engine template gained its per-face splice points (engine/objects/collision.emp
// `solid_face_geometry` + the three default face bodies), so a face that
// launches is now a face that does not splice its solid default. What the
// engine reads is the spring's own VELOCITY PAIR, never its subtype: Spring_Init
// decodes the subtype into (x_vel, y_vel) once at spawn and the engine's
// Touch_Spring asks only which way that vector points. An unimplemented
// direction decodes to (0, 0), which is a plain solid block on all four faces —
// so the release shape degrades to "a spring that does nothing" rather than to
// a random impulse, and the DEBUG shape asserts on it in Spring_Init.
//
// WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT PORTED, and why:
//   * the diagonals (SPRING_DIR_UPDIAG / _DOWNDIAG). They additionally set
//     ground_vel and flip_angle, which is a different physics problem again;
//     they keep S3K's direction numbers so they cannot be taken by anything
//     else, and decode to the inert (0, 0) vector until someone builds them;
//   * the horizontal-only subtype extras — bit 7 "kill y_vel on launch"
//     (:47913-47915), bit 0 "twirl" with bit 1 doubling as its flip count
//     (:47917-47926), and bits 3-2 "switch the player's solid layer"
//     (:47934-47946). The horizontal spring has now arrived without them, so
//     they are three independent riders rather than one package; booked on SP-5
//     in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md. Bit 7 is the one with real gameplay weight (it
//     is what stops a side spring converting a fall into a diagonal), bits 3-2
//     need a solid-layer system this engine does not have yet, and bit 0's
//     twirl is a pure animation flourish that also RE-READS bit 1 as its flip
//     count — so building it would put two meanings on the strength bit;
//   * the 2P/Competition_mode variants and the reverse-gravity arms.
//
// THE SUBTYPE BIT LAYOUT, read off sonic3k.asm rather than off a summary:
//
//   bits 6-4   DIRECTION, an index into Spring_Index (:47520-47525, five
//              entries: Up, Horizontal, Down, UpDiag, DownDiag). Obj_Spring
//              decodes it at :47512-47514 as `move.b subtype,d0 / lsr.w #3,d0 /
//              andi.w #$E,d0`, which yields the index ALREADY DOUBLED for a
//              word table — i.e. dir = (subtype >> 4) & 7. It is easy to misread
//              that pair as a bit-3 field; it is not.
//   bit 1      STRENGTH: clear = red, set = yellow. Spring_Common at
//              :47641-47643 does `andi.w #2,d0` and uses the RESULT as the byte
//              offset into the two-word table at :47654-47656, which is the
//              whole reason the field sits on bit 1 rather than bit 0. Ours
//              exploits the same identity — see Spring_Init.
//   bits 3-2,
//   bit 0,
//   bit 7      horizontal-only extras, listed above.
//
// LEFT vs RIGHT IS NOT IN S3K'S SUBTYPE, AND IT IS IN OURS. This is the layout's
// one genuine surprise and the one place SP-5 deliberately diverges.
//
// What the reference does: a horizontal spring's facing is the OBJECT's x-flip
// status bit. sub_23190 launches rightward and then, at `btst #0,status(a0)`
// (:47898), negates the velocity and clears the player's facing if the bit is
// clear. So S3K has exactly ONE horizontal direction value, and its two visible
// springs are one subtype placed with and without the flip. This engine already
// carries a placement flip into both render_flags and status (Load_Object), so
// following it was available and cost nothing.
//
// WE DO NOT FOLLOW IT. Left and right get their own direction values, and the
// x-flip stays purely visual — nothing in Spring_Init or Touch_Spring reads it.
// Three reasons, in the order they decided it:
//
//   1. THE FLIP IS INVISIBLE ON THIS SPRING. Our placeholder art is a symmetric
//      16x16 square (see "ART: NONE OF ITS OWN" below), so a flip changes
//      nothing on screen. S3K's designer flipping a spring sees it turn round;
//      ours would tick a checkbox with no feedback and find out which way it
//      points by walking into it. Silently-wrong level data is the exact failure
//      this file's ensures exist to prevent, and the flip model manufactures it.
//   2. IT IS THE ONLY DIRECTION THE PICKER COULD NOT NAME. The published channel
//      is `ObjSub_Spring__<Name>` and it carries subtype VALUES; under S3K's
//      model the honest name is `ObjSub_Spring__Horizontal_Red`, and which way
//      it actually fires lives in a different widget. Four directions in one
//      sorted list, each meaning one thing, is the whole point of the channel.
//   3. IT COSTS NOTHING. The direction field is 3 bits wide and S3K uses five of
//      its eight values, so LEFT takes a value that was already there and unused.
//
// The price is that a hypothetical S3K level-data importer would have to consult
// the flip bit and rewrite subtype $10 into $10 or $50. That is a converter's
// job, it is one line of it, and no such converter exists.
//
// THE THREE REFERENCE NUMBERS, quoted:
//   launch velocity  -$1000   sonic3k.asm:47655   (word_22EF0's first entry,
//                                                  selected by subtype bit 1
//                                                  CLEAR at :47641-47643)
//   fire animation   dc.b 0, 1, 0, 0, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, $FD, 0
//                    General/Sprites/Level Misc/Anim - Spring.asm:9
//   idle animation   dc.b $F, 0, $FF        same file:8
//   sound            sfx_Spring = $B1        sonic3k.constants.asm:1304,
//                                            played at sonic3k.asm:47768-47769
//
// A FOURTH NUMBER DOES NOT EXIST: there is no cooldown constant. S3K's vertical
// spring holds no timer anywhere between Obj_Spring_Up (:47659-47684) and
// sub_22F98 (:47717-47770); re-firing is interlocked by the contact test alone.
// Ours is interlocked the same way — see Touch_Spring's header.
//
// THE SOUND IS NOT WIRED, and the omission is loud rather than quiet: sfx $B1 is
// not in this game's SFX bank, and adding it is a multi-commit lane, not a
// constant (the transcoder refuses `smpsModOff`, which no shipped SFX uses; and
// any byte added to the SFX bank blob moves the ROM and takes sigil's
// repin/refreeze ritual). Booked in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md. When it lands, the
// spring needs no code change: an `AF_SOUND, SFXID_SPRING` pair goes in front of
// the fire script's first frame byte and the animator plays it exactly once per
// launch, because the launch is what restarts the script.
// ===========================================================================

// The launch impulses, 8.8 fixed point (-$1000 = -16.0 px/frame), written in
// their UP sense because that is how S3K's table holds them. Spring_Init picks
// one by the subtype's strength bit and the direction decides its axis and sign
// (Spring_Launch, below), so a yellow spring and a leftward one are both placed
// SUBTYPES rather than extra objdefs.
pub const SPRING_RED_VELOCITY    = -$1000       // sonic3k.asm:47655 (word_22EF0[0])
pub const SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY = -$A00        // sonic3k.asm:47656 (word_22EF0[1])

// The horizontal launch's input freeze, S3K's `move.w #$F, $32(a1)` at
// sonic3k.asm:47905-47906. Vertical launches take no lock in S3K or here — the
// player is airborne and the ground input arm is not running.
pub const SPRING_MOVE_LOCK = $F                 // sonic3k.asm:47905-47906

// -----------------------------------------------
// THE SUBTYPE ENCODING — one table, one encoder, one published block.
//
// Every name below is generated from `spring_subtype()`; no published value is a
// hand-written literal. That is deliberate and it is what makes the encoding
// safe to change: move a field and every name moves with it, or none does.
// -----------------------------------------------
const SPRING_SUBTYPE_DIR_SHIFT = 4      // bits 6-4 — sonic3k.asm:47512-47514
const SPRING_SUBTYPE_DIR_MASK  = 7
const SPRING_SUBTYPE_WEAK_BIT  = 1      // bit 1    — sonic3k.asm:47641-47643

// Direction values. The first five are the order of S3K's Spring_Index
// (:47520-47525) and they keep those numbers whether or not we act on them — a
// direction must take the number it already has rather than a convenient one.
//
// SPRING_DIR_RIGHT IS S3K'S "HORIZONTAL" SLOT, renamed rather than re-numbered,
// and rightward is the honest reading of that entry: sub_23190 builds a
// rightward velocity and only then negates it under the flip bit (:47898), so
// value 1 IS the un-negated rightward case. SPRING_DIR_LEFT is 5 — the first
// value S3K's five-entry table does not use — which is the divergence described
// under "LEFT vs RIGHT" above.
const SPRING_DIR_UP        = 0
const SPRING_DIR_RIGHT     = 1          // S3K's "Horizontal", un-negated sense
const SPRING_DIR_DOWN      = 2
const SPRING_DIR_UPDIAG    = 3          // not implemented — decodes to (0, 0)
const SPRING_DIR_DOWNDIAG  = 4          // not implemented — decodes to (0, 0)
const SPRING_DIR_LEFT      = 5          // OURS, not S3K's — see "LEFT vs RIGHT"

// Spring_Launch's shape, named because Spring_Init's index arithmetic is folded
// against both numbers and the ensures below pin the fold to them.
// One ENTRY is the (x_vel, y_vel) launch vector for one direction at one
// strength; one ROW is a direction's two entries, red then yellow.
const SPRING_LAUNCH_ENTRY_SIZE = 4      // two words: x_vel, y_vel
const SPRING_LAUNCH_ROW_SIZE   = 8      // red entry + yellow entry

// `weak` is 0 for red, 1 for yellow — the sense of S3K's bit, not inverted.
// Flat expression, no block-tail `if` (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §1).
comptime fn spring_subtype(dir: int, weak: int) -> int {
    return (dir << SPRING_SUBTYPE_DIR_SHIFT) | (weak << SPRING_SUBTYPE_WEAK_BIT)
}

// THE PUBLISHED SUBTYPE NAMES — oracle's object picker channel.
//
// `ObjSub_<Object>__<Name>`: the picker takes the object name it already has
// from `ObjDef_Spring`, builds the prefix `ObjSub_Spring__`, prefix-searches and
// keeps whatever follows whole. It never splits on an underscore, so the
// DOUBLE underscore is doing one job — being a sequence an object name cannot
// contain. With a single one, a future `ObjDef_Spring_Board` would emit
// `ObjSub_Spring_Board__Up` and file its subtypes under THIS object. Internal
// single underscores are therefore free, and direction leads so a listing sorts
// and scans by the axis the picker is for.
//
// `pub equ`, NOT `pub const`, and the difference is the whole deliverable: a
// `const` in an ordinary module emits NOTHING — no equate row, no symbol row
// (measured: this file's own `pub const SPRING_RED_VELOCITY` has no row in any
// listing, while `RING_BUFFER_ENTRY_SIZE` does only because
// engine/system/constants.emp is one of sigil's harvested files). A `pub equ`
// lands in the listing's Equate Table, where `lookup_equate` can see it and
// `lookup_symbol` cannot. Ask the wrong one and the refusal is
// indistinguishable from the name not existing.
//
// ONLY THE IMPLEMENTED SUBTYPES ARE PUBLISHED. Naming a direction the engine
// asserts on would put a broken choice in the owner's picker — so the two
// diagonals have no names here, and will get them in the commit that makes them
// launch.
//
// DIRECTION FIRST, STRENGTH LAST, so this sorted list scans by the axis the
// picker is for: all four Downs, then Lefts, then Rights, then Ups.
pub equ ObjSub_Spring__Down_Red     = spring_subtype(SPRING_DIR_DOWN, 0)
pub equ ObjSub_Spring__Down_Yellow  = spring_subtype(SPRING_DIR_DOWN, 1)
pub equ ObjSub_Spring__Left_Red     = spring_subtype(SPRING_DIR_LEFT, 0)
pub equ ObjSub_Spring__Left_Yellow  = spring_subtype(SPRING_DIR_LEFT, 1)
pub equ ObjSub_Spring__Right_Red    = spring_subtype(SPRING_DIR_RIGHT, 0)
pub equ ObjSub_Spring__Right_Yellow = spring_subtype(SPRING_DIR_RIGHT, 1)
pub equ ObjSub_Spring__Up_Red       = spring_subtype(SPRING_DIR_UP, 0)
pub equ ObjSub_Spring__Up_Yellow    = spring_subtype(SPRING_DIR_UP, 1)

// THE ALREADY-PLACED SPRINGS MUST NOT MOVE. OJZ act 1 section 0 places three
// springs with placement word $0000, i.e. subtype 0, and this is the guard that
// subtype 0 still decodes to the exact behaviour they had before the decode
// existed: up, red. An encoder that put direction or strength anywhere else
// would silently re-aim live level data.
ensure(spring_subtype(SPRING_DIR_UP, 0) == 0,
       "subtype 0 must stay `up, red` — the springs already placed in OJZ act 1 carry placement word $0000 and would be silently re-aimed")
// The weak bit's VALUE is its byte offset into a table of WORD entries, which is
// why S3K put strength on bit 1 (:47641-47643). Ours are 4-byte (x, y) entries,
// so Spring_Init doubles the masked bit once — `add.w d1, d1` — and still needs
// no shift-and-scale pair. State it, because the code depends on it.
ensure((1 << SPRING_SUBTYPE_WEAK_BIT) * 2 == SPRING_LAUNCH_ENTRY_SIZE,
       "the strength bit must weigh half a Spring_Launch entry — Spring_Init doubles the masked bit ONCE to reach the strength's row, and any other weight silently selects the wrong velocity")
// The direction decode is `lsr.w #1 / andi.w #$38`, which folds the >>4 shift
// and the *8 row scale into one shift because they differ by exactly 3 and the
// mask cleans up the rest. That fold is only correct at shift 4 with a 3-bit
// field and 8-byte rows, so pin all three together rather than leaving a reader
// to spot why the constant below is not SPRING_SUBTYPE_DIR_SHIFT.
ensure((SPRING_SUBTYPE_DIR_SHIFT == 4) && (SPRING_SUBTYPE_DIR_MASK == 7) && ((SPRING_SUBTYPE_DIR_MASK + 1) * SPRING_LAUNCH_ROW_SIZE == 64),
       "Spring_Init's `lsr.w #1 / andi.w #$38` fold assumes direction at shift 4, a 3-bit field, and 8-byte Spring_Launch rows")
// The direction field must not overlap the strength bit or the horizontal-only
// extras in bits 3-0. A shift below 4 would make a yellow spring read as a
// different direction.
ensure(SPRING_SUBTYPE_DIR_SHIFT >= 4 && ((SPRING_SUBTYPE_DIR_MASK << SPRING_SUBTYPE_DIR_SHIFT) & $0F) == 0,
       "the direction field must sit clear of bits 3-0 — bit 1 is the strength and bits 3-2/0 are S3K's horizontal-only extras")

// Animation ids, indexing Ani_Spring's offset table.
pub const SPRING_ANIM_IDLE = 0
pub const SPRING_ANIM_FIRE = 1

// Mapping frame ids, indexing Map_Spring.
const SPRING_FRAME_IDLE   = 0
const SPRING_FRAME_SQUASH = 1
const SPRING_FRAME_EXTEND = 2

// -----------------------------------------------
// Spring_Launched — bound to Game.spring_launched (config/game.emp).
//
// Touch_Spring has already seated the player against the launching face and
// written the impulse into the matching velocity field. What is left is what
// only the game can say: what the impulse MEANS for the player's state machine,
// and that the spring should visibly react.
//
// TWO TRANSITIONS, NOT ONE, AND THE SPRING'S OWN x_vel IS THE DISCRIMINATOR.
// A vertical launch (up or down) makes the player airborne; a HORIZONTAL launch
// leaves him exactly as he was — running if he was running — and instead
// rewrites the grounded driver. That is S3K's split too, and it is not a detail:
// a horizontal spring that forced PSTATE_AIR would launch a running player into
// a helpless mid-air slide, and one that only set x_vel would be undone on the
// same tick, because Ground_Move_Cap re-derives x_vel from PlayerV.ground_speed
// every frame (which is the whole reason Game.solid_pushed exists).
//
// Reading the SPRING's x_vel rather than the player's is deliberate: the player
// may be carrying any x_vel of his own into a vertical launch, so his is not a
// question about the spring. The spring's velocity pair is the launch vector and
// exactly one of its two words is nonzero.
//
// In:  a2 = player SST, a3 = spring SST
// Out: none (tail-jumps Player_SetState on the vertical arm)
// Clobbers: d0-d2, a0-a2 — the hook's declared bound: d0 carries the state,
//   a0 carries the player into Player_SetState's slot, and Player_SetState
//   itself clobbers d1-d2/a1-a2 (its own plus its enter hook's).
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Spring_Launched (a2: *Sst, a3: *Sst) clobbers(d0-d2/a0-a2) {
        // Restart the fire animation. Writing `anim` ALONE is not enough and the
        // bug it would hide is subtle: AnimateSprite's change detection is
        // `anim != prev_anim`, so a spring hit again while still playing its
        // extend frames would take the unchanged path and simply carry on from
        // wherever it was — the second bounce would launch the player silently,
        // with no visible spring motion. S3K forces the same restart in one word
        // write because its anim/prev_anim are adjacent (sonic3k.asm:47718,
        // `move.w #1<<8, anim(a0)`); ours sit at $18 and $20, so it is two byte
        // writes that say what they mean.
        move.b  #SPRING_ANIM_FIRE, Sst.anim(a3)
        move.b  #$FF, Sst.prev_anim(a3)

        move.w  Sst.x_vel(a3), d0
        bne     .horizontal

        // PSTATE_AIR or PSTATE_AIRBALL, NEVER PSTATE_JUMP. The pair
        // {PSTATE_JUMP, PSTATE_ROLLJUMP} is this engine's exact equivalent of
        // S3K's `jumping` byte, and it is what grants Sonic the insta-shield;
        // player_instashield.emp enumerates every writer of player_state to
        // establish that, and names THIS site — "springs, badnik bounces and
        // knockback ... when they are built they must install PSTATE_AIR /
        // PSTATE_AIRBALL" — as the one that would break it. S3K agrees from the
        // other side: sub_22F98 does `clr.b jumping(a1)` (sonic3k.asm:47728).
        //
        // Curl is PRESERVED across the launch: S3K's launcher never touches its
        // rolling status bit either, and keeping it spares a rolling player the
        // uncurl y-shift PHook_EnsureStanding would otherwise apply on top of
        // the seat Touch_Spring just wrote. Same one-decision shape as
        // Ground_DetachState (player_ground.emp).
        moveq   #PSTATE_AIR, d0
        btst    #ST_ROLLING, Sst.status(a2)
        beq     .set_state
        moveq   #PSTATE_AIRBALL, d0
    .set_state:
        movea.l a2, a0                  // Player_SetState takes the player in a0
        jbra    Player_SetState

    .horizontal:
        // THE GROUNDED DRIVER IS THE LAUNCH. Touch_Spring already wrote the
        // engine-owned x_vel, which is what moves an AIRBORNE player; a grounded
        // one is moved by his inertia, and Ground_Move_Cap rebuilds x_vel from it
        // every tick, so without this store the launch would be erased on the
        // frame after it happened. Same field, same reason, as Solid_Pushed.
        move.w  d0, PlayerV.ground_speed(a2)

        // THE CONTROL LOCK, S3K's sonic3k.asm:47905-47906. Without it the player
        // is holding a direction at the moment he is launched — that is how he
        // reached the spring — and his very next grounded tick applies the brake
        // against his new speed. The lock freezes the input's brake/facing arm
        // only; friction, gravity and the terrain still run
        // (games/sonic4/player/player_ground.emp, and see SLIDE_GETUP_LOCK for
        // the same $F on the glide get-up).
        move.w  #SPRING_MOVE_LOCK, PlayerV.move_lock(a2)

        // FACE THE WAY HE IS THROWN. S3K writes the facing on this path too
        // (:47898, the same `btst` that chooses the sign). Cheaper to clear and
        // conditionally set than to branch around two stores, and the pose the
        // animator picks is derived from this bit.
        bclr    #ST_XFLIP, Sst.status(a2)
        tst.w   d0
        bpl     .faced
        bset    #ST_XFLIP, Sst.status(a2)       // launched left = facing left
    .faced:
        // NO STATE CHANGE, deliberately: a running player keeps running and an
        // airborne one stays airborne. S3K's horizontal spring likewise never
        // touches `jumping` — it is the vertical arms that clear it.
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Spring_Launch — the whole subtype decode as one table: for every one of the
// eight direction values, at both strengths, the (x_vel, y_vel) pair the spring
// carries for the rest of its life. 8 rows x 2 entries x 2 words = 64 bytes.
//
// It replaces S3K's word_22EF0 (:47654-47656) rather than transcribing it,
// because S3K's table is a MAGNITUDE table read by five separate per-direction
// entry points that each know their own axis and sign. One table that already
// holds the axis and the sign collapses those five entry points into the single
// `move.l` in Spring_Init, and it is what lets the engine stay ignorant of
// spring directions entirely (engine/objects/collision.emp's Touch_Spring reads
// the vector, never the subtype).
//
// NOT ONE LITERAL IN IT. Every cell comes from spring_axis_x/spring_axis_y
// applied to the two reference velocities, so the table cannot disagree with the
// SPRING_DIR_* constants: move SPRING_DIR_LEFT to another value and its row
// moves with it. A hand-written table of 32 signed words would be four rows of
// sign errors waiting to happen, and no ensure could tell a swapped pair from a
// deliberate one.
//
// THE UNIMPLEMENTED DIRECTIONS ARE (0, 0) AND THAT IS THE SAFE DEGRADATION.
// Touch_Spring launches on the face its vector points at; a zero vector points
// at no face, so every face takes the ordinary solid default and the object is a
// plain block. That is what the release shape does with a direction it cannot
// fly (the DEBUG shape asserts first), and it is why the sentinel is a zero
// vector rather than an out-of-range marker the release shape would have to
// branch on.
// -----------------------------------------------

// The launch vector's two axes, per direction, at a given magnitude. `v` is the
// signed reference velocity in its UP sense (negative — S3K's springs are
// written as upward impulses), so DOWN and RIGHT negate it and UP and LEFT do
// not. Flat accumulators over statement ifs, never a nested if-expression
// (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §1 — the silent unit fold).
comptime fn spring_axis_x(dir: int, v: int) -> int {
    comptime var r = 0
    if dir == SPRING_DIR_RIGHT { r = -v }       // -(-$1000) = +$1000, rightward
    if dir == SPRING_DIR_LEFT  { r = v }
    return r
}
comptime fn spring_axis_y(dir: int, v: int) -> int {
    comptime var r = 0
    if dir == SPRING_DIR_UP   { r = v }
    if dir == SPRING_DIR_DOWN { r = -v }
    return r
}

pub data Spring_Launch: [i16; 32] = [
    // dir 0 — UP                  red                                              yellow
    spring_axis_x(0, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(0, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY),
    spring_axis_x(0, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(0, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY),
    // dir 1 — RIGHT
    spring_axis_x(1, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(1, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY),
    spring_axis_x(1, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(1, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY),
    // dir 2 — DOWN
    spring_axis_x(2, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(2, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY),
    spring_axis_x(2, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(2, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY),
    // dir 3 — UPDIAG (not implemented — inert)
    spring_axis_x(3, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(3, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY),
    spring_axis_x(3, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(3, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY),
    // dir 4 — DOWNDIAG (not implemented — inert)
    spring_axis_x(4, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(4, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY),
    spring_axis_x(4, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(4, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY),
    // dir 5 — LEFT
    spring_axis_x(5, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(5, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY),
    spring_axis_x(5, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(5, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY),
    // dir 6 — unused by S3K and by us (inert)
    spring_axis_x(6, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(6, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY),
    spring_axis_x(6, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(6, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY),
    // dir 7 — unused by S3K and by us (inert)
    spring_axis_x(7, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(7, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY),
    spring_axis_x(7, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY), spring_axis_y(7, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY),
]

// The four implemented directions must each launch along ONE axis, in the right
// sense, at the reference magnitude. Written as four independent facts rather
// than one loop, because the failure this catches is a single row's sign.
ensure((spring_axis_x(SPRING_DIR_UP, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY) == 0) && (spring_axis_y(SPRING_DIR_UP, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY) == SPRING_RED_VELOCITY),
       "the UP spring must launch straight up at the reference velocity")
ensure((spring_axis_y(SPRING_DIR_DOWN, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY) == -SPRING_RED_VELOCITY) && (spring_axis_x(SPRING_DIR_DOWN, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY) == 0),
       "the DOWN spring must launch straight down — the UP velocity negated, S3K writes both as one magnitude")
ensure((spring_axis_x(SPRING_DIR_RIGHT, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY) == -SPRING_RED_VELOCITY) && (spring_axis_y(SPRING_DIR_RIGHT, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY) == 0),
       "the RIGHT spring must launch in +x — Touch_Spring matches the sign of x_vel against which side the player is on, so a sign error here makes the spring fire out of its own back")
ensure((spring_axis_x(SPRING_DIR_LEFT, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY) == SPRING_RED_VELOCITY) && (spring_axis_y(SPRING_DIR_LEFT, SPRING_RED_VELOCITY) == 0),
       "the LEFT spring must launch in -x")
// The inert sentinel, stated for BOTH strengths: a nonzero cell in an
// unimplemented row is a spring that fires in the release shape, where the
// DEBUG assert that would have caught it does not exist.
ensure((spring_axis_x(SPRING_DIR_UPDIAG, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY) == 0) && (spring_axis_y(SPRING_DIR_UPDIAG, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY) == 0) && (spring_axis_x(SPRING_DIR_DOWNDIAG, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY) == 0) && (spring_axis_y(SPRING_DIR_DOWNDIAG, SPRING_YELLOW_VELOCITY) == 0),
       "an unimplemented direction must decode to a ZERO vector — that is what makes it a plain solid block in the release shape instead of a random impulse")

// -----------------------------------------------
// Spring_Init — decode the subtype, then become Spring_Main.
//
// Runs once, on the frame Load_Object spawns the object: `code:` in the ObjDef
// names the INIT routine, which patches code_addr to the Main routine, exactly
// as TestSolid_Init does. Load_Object has already written the placement subtype
// into the SST ($19) and carried the placement's x-flip into render_flags AND
// status, so both halves of a spring's identity are readable here.
//
// THE WHOLE DECODE IS ONE INDEX AND ONE `move.l`. Direction and strength are
// two disjoint bit fields, and Spring_Launch is indexed by both at once, so
// there is no branch anywhere in here and no per-direction entry point — which
// is the thing S3K spends five routines on (Spring_Index, :47520-47525).
//
// The index folds three operations into two instructions:
//   direction * 8   =  (subtype >> 4) * 8  =  (subtype >> 1) & $38
//   strength  * 4   =  (subtype & 2) * 2   =  the masked bit, doubled once
// The first identity works only because the shift and the row scale differ by
// exactly 3; the second only because S3K put strength on bit 1 rather than bit 0
// (:47641-47643). Both are pinned by ensures above rather than left as
// coincidences a reader has to spot.
//
// THE PAIR IS WRITTEN AS TWO WORD MOVES AND NOT ONE `move.l`, and the reason is
// worth stating because the long looks obviously better. Sst.x_vel ($0A) and
// Sst.y_vel ($0C) are adjacent and both even, so the long access is legal 68000
// and two bytes cheaper — but sigil refuses it: `[operand.field-overrun] .l
// access reads 4 bytes but field `x_vel` is 2 bytes`. That check is right and
// worth more than the two bytes: a `.l` through a typed field name is exactly
// how a struct reorder turns into a silent overwrite of the field after it.
// The DEBUG assert below DOES use `move.l`, because there it reads the TABLE
// (an untyped address), not a field.
//
// BOTH VELOCITY FIELDS ARE THE SPRING'S LAUNCH VECTOR, and they stay the only
// thing the engine reads (engine/objects/collision.emp's Touch_Spring). That
// keeps the engine seam exactly where the vertical spring put it — the engine
// knows nothing about red or yellow, or about up or left — while the CHOICE
// lives in the placement. It is also why a yellow spring is a subtype and not a
// second objdef: the ObjDef carries neither velocity.
//
// THE ASSERT IS THE POINT OF THE DEBUG BLOCK, not the decode: the decode is now
// unconditional, because four directions are live and the release shape must
// perform it. What DEBUG adds is the reading of a ZERO vector as "this placement
// names a direction this engine cannot fly", which stops the build's own assert
// handler instead of leaving a spring that silently does nothing. Silently-wrong
// level data is exactly what a subtype channel makes easy.
//
// In:  a0 = own SST (base fields set by Load_Object)
// Clobbers: d0-d3, a1-a2 — the init work (d0/d1/a1) inside Spring_Main's set,
//   which is what a caller sees because this falls into it.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Spring_Init (a0: *Sst) clobbers(d0-d3/a1-a2) preserves(a0) falls_into Spring_Main {
        moveq   #0, d0
        move.b  Sst.subtype(a0), d0
        move.w  d0, d1

        // --- direction: (subtype >> 4) * SPRING_LAUNCH_ROW_SIZE ---
        lsr.w   #1, d0
        andi.w  #$38, d0

        // --- strength: the masked bit, doubled to an entry offset ---
        andi.w  #1<<SPRING_SUBTYPE_WEAK_BIT, d1
        add.w   d1, d1
        add.w   d1, d0                  // d0 = byte offset into Spring_Launch

        lea     Spring_Launch(pc), a1
        move.w  (a1,d0.w), Sst.x_vel(a0)
        move.w  2(a1,d0.w), Sst.y_vel(a0)

        // A zero vector is the table's "no such direction" sentinel — inert by
        // construction in the release shape (Touch_Spring launches on no face,
        // so the object is a plain solid block), and a hard stop here.
        if DEBUG == 1 {
        move.l  (a1,d0.w), d1
        assert.l d1, ne, #0
        }

        move.w  #Spring_Main - ObjCodeBase, Sst.code_addr(a0)
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Spring_Main — animate and draw. Solidity and the launch are TouchResponse's
// (engine/objects/collision.emp), exactly as TestSolid_Main's are, so this is
// the same two-call body plus the animator.
//
// In:  a0 = own SST
// Clobbers: d0-d3, a1-a2 — AnimateSprite (d0-d2/a1-a2) union Draw_Sprite
//   (d0-d3/a1).
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Spring_Main (a0: *Sst) clobbers(d0-d3/a1-a2) preserves(a0) {
        jbsr    AnimateSprite
        jbra    Draw_Sprite
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Mapping frames.
//
// ART: NONE OF ITS OWN — these three frames draw out of the resident test-object
// block at VRAM_TEST_OBJ, the same 8 tiles ObjDef_Solid / ObjDef_Static /
// ObjDef_PathSwap already draw from, so the spring costs ZERO new VRAM. That is
// not the art anyone wants and it is not a silent substitution: S3K's vertical
// spring needs 12 tiles (the union of frames 0/1/2 in Map - Spring.asm — tiles
// 0-3, 8-9 and $A-$F of its 16-tile block at art base $4A4), and
// games/sonic4/vram.toml has exactly ONE free tile in the whole 2048-tile map
// (the [[free]] run at base 959). The numbers, the carve options and the
// recommendation are in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md; a real spring sheet is a
// vram.toml edit plus a regenerate, and only these three lines change here.
//
// The three frames are the two colour squares of that block read as spring
// states: idle sits centred, squash swaps colour and sinks 4px, extend swaps
// back and lifts 12px. Distinct enough to see the object fire from across the
// screen, which is what the frames are for until the art exists.
const SPRING_TILE_A = 0                 // test block's first 16x16 square (tiles 0-3)
const SPRING_TILE_B = 4                 // its second colour (tiles 4-7)

pub offsets Map_Spring {
    Idle: MapFrame1 = MapFrame1{
        x_min: -8, x_max: 8, y_min: -8, y_max: 8, piece_count: 1,
        piece: piece(x: -8, y: -8, w: 2, h: 2, tile: SPRING_TILE_A) },
    Squash: MapFrame1 = MapFrame1{
        x_min: -8, x_max: 8, y_min: -4, y_max: 12, piece_count: 1,
        piece: piece(x: -8, y: -4, w: 2, h: 2, tile: SPRING_TILE_B) },
    Extend: MapFrame1 = MapFrame1{
        x_min: -8, x_max: 8, y_min: -20, y_max: -4, piece_count: 1,
        piece: piece(x: -8, y: -20, w: 2, h: 2, tile: SPRING_TILE_A) },
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Animation scripts.
//
// IDLE — S3K byte_2372E, `dc.b $F, 0, $FF`: hold frame 0 for $F+1 frames, loop.
// The trailing pad byte is NEVER READ (AF_END restarts the script from byte 1
// before the cursor could reach it); it is there so this item is EVEN-sized and
// the MapFrame1 records above stay word-aligned whatever order the placer packs
// them in. Stated rather than left as a mystery byte.
const SPRING_IDLE_SCRIPT: [u8; 4] = [$F, SPRING_FRAME_IDLE, AF_END, 0]

// FIRE — S3K byte_23731 transcribed, Anim - Spring.asm:9:
//   dc.b 0, 1, 0, 0, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, $FD, 0
// Duration 0 = one display frame per entry (both S3K's Animate_Sprite and this
// engine's show a frame for duration+1 frames), so: squash for 1, idle for 2,
// extend for 6, then AF_CHANGE back to the idle animation. Nine display frames
// of visible reaction, which is also how long the spring looks busy — but NOT
// how long it is busy: nothing in the fire path blocks a re-launch, in S3K or
// here. The interlock is the falling test in Touch_Spring.
const SPRING_FIRE_SCRIPT: [u8; 12] = [
    0,
    SPRING_FRAME_SQUASH,
    SPRING_FRAME_IDLE, SPRING_FRAME_IDLE,
    SPRING_FRAME_EXTEND, SPRING_FRAME_EXTEND, SPRING_FRAME_EXTEND,
    SPRING_FRAME_EXTEND, SPRING_FRAME_EXTEND, SPRING_FRAME_EXTEND,
    AF_CHANGE, SPRING_ANIM_IDLE,
]

// The reference's display-frame count, re-derived from OUR bytes rather than
// asserted: count the frame bytes after the duration byte and multiply by the
// hold. S3K's script shows 9 x (0+1) = 9. A transcription that dropped or
// duplicated a frame byte fails here by name, which is the failure this guard
// exists for — the bytes above are hand-copied from a listing.
pub const S3K_SPRING_FIRE_FRAMES = 9    // frame bytes in Anim - Spring.asm:9
pub const S3K_SPRING_FIRE_HOLD   = 0    // its duration byte

// Count frame bytes from byte 1 up to the FIRST control code, then multiply by
// the hold. Stopping at the control code is the load-bearing part: this script's
// terminator is the two-byte `AF_CHANGE, id` pair, and the id is an ORDINARY
// byte value ($00) that a bare "is it below the control threshold" test counts
// as a tenth frame. It did, on the first build of this file — the guard below
// fired with 10-vs-9 before this `live` flag existed, which is the only reason
// the count is right now. (ring_sparkle's equivalent has no such flag because
// its terminator, AF_DELETE, carries no argument.)
//
// Flat accumulators over STATEMENT ifs, never a nested if-expression
// (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §1 — the silent unit fold).
comptime fn spring_display_frames(script: [u8; 12]) -> int {
    comptime var frames = 0
    comptime var live = 1
    for i in 1..12 {
        if script[i] >= AF_SET_FIELD { live = 0 }      // $F7+ = control/event
        if live == 1 { frames = frames + 1 }
    }
    return frames * (script[0] + 1)
}

ensure(spring_display_frames(SPRING_FIRE_SCRIPT) == S3K_SPRING_FIRE_FRAMES * (S3K_SPRING_FIRE_HOLD + 1),
       "the spring's fire script shows {spring_display_frames(SPRING_FIRE_SCRIPT)} display frames; S3K's Ani_Spring anim 1 shows {S3K_SPRING_FIRE_FRAMES * (S3K_SPRING_FIRE_HOLD + 1)} (9 frame bytes at duration 0) — re-derive the script from Anim - Spring.asm:9, do not retune the reference")
ensure(SPRING_FIRE_SCRIPT[10] == AF_CHANGE && SPRING_FIRE_SCRIPT[11] == SPRING_ANIM_IDLE,
       "the fire script must end by switching back to the idle animation — AF_CHANGE to any other id, or a missing terminator, leaves the spring stuck extended")
ensure(SPRING_IDLE_SCRIPT[2] == AF_END,
       "the idle script must LOOP — without the terminator the animator walks off the end of the script into the fire script's bytes")

pub offsets Ani_Spring {
    Idle: [u8; 4]  = SPRING_IDLE_SCRIPT,        // SPRING_ANIM_IDLE
    Fire: [u8; 12] = SPRING_FIRE_SCRIPT,        // SPRING_ANIM_FIRE
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// The archetype. width/height are the FULL box in THIS engine (the AABB combines
// player.width + target.width and halves the sum), so 16x16 is the spring's own
// 16x16 footprint — the box that matches its placeholder art, and the same box
// ObjDef_Solid declares.
//
// IT IS NOT S3K'S BOX, and the two are easy to conflate because the engines mean
// different things by the same field name. S3K's `width_pixels` is a HALF-width:
// Obj_Spring writes $10 (sonic3k.asm:47507) and Obj_Spring_Up then passes
// `move.w #$1B, d1` (:47660) = that 16 PLUS Sonic's own 11px half-width, so S3K's
// vertical spring is 32 PX WIDE and 16 tall (its `move.w #8, d2` half-height,
// :47661) — which matches its 4-tile-wide top plate in Map - Spring.asm. Ours is
// 16 wide because its placeholder art is one 16x16 square. Raise `width` to 32 in
// the same commit that gives the spring real 32px art: the box should follow the
// art, not the reference's field name. (I had this backwards in the first draft
// of this comment and caught it only by re-reading :47660 against the mapping.)
//
// NO x_vel AND NO y_vel HERE, and their absence is the point. The pair is the
// spring's LAUNCH VECTOR — still the only thing Touch_Spring reads — but it is
// written by Spring_Init from the placed subtype, so the archetype must NOT
// pre-seed either. Leaving `y_vel: SPRING_RED_VELOCITY` behind would have been
// harmless (the init overwrites it on the first frame) and would also have been
// a lie: it would read as the authority while a yellow or sideways spring
// quietly disagreed with it, and the one frame between spawn and init is the
// window where the two could differ. The spring itself never moves — nothing in
// Spring_Main integrates velocity — so both fields are free for this.
//
// `code:` NAMES THE INIT, not Main. Spring_Init patches code_addr on its one and
// only frame, exactly as ObjDef_Solid's TestSolid_Init does.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub data ObjDef_Spring: ObjDef = objdef(
    code: "Spring_Init", map: "Map_Spring", art: vram_art(VRAM_TEST_OBJ),
    priority: 3, width: 16, height: 16, collision: COLLISION_SPRING,
    anim_table: extern("Ani_Spring"))
